# Git Commits

## Format

- All git commits generated by the agent MUST use the
  [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org) format:
  `type(scope): description`.
- Allowed types include: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore` (and
  other standard Conventional Commits types as appropriate, e.g. `docs`,
  `perf`, `build`, `ci`).

## Description

- The description must concisely and accurately describe the exact change
  made, prioritizing clarity for human reviewers over brevity.
- Avoid vague messages (e.g. "update code", "fix stuff").

## Scopes

- Use a scope to indicate the modified module or layer, e.g.:
  - `feat(telephony):` for `data/telephony` changes
  - `fix(ui):` for `ui/screens` changes
  - `test(mappers):` for mapper unit tests
- Omit the scope only when a change is truly cross-cutting and no single
  module/layer applies.

## Atomic Commits

- Autonomous subagents must create atomic commits: one commit per logical
  change.
- Never group unrelated modifications (e.g. a feature change plus an
  unrelated refactor or formatting pass) into a single commit.
